WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.700
SpaceX gains classified Pentagon tracking data to sharpen collision avoidance for its 11,000 satellites. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 110. The agreement gives SpaceX access to the US Space Force's most detailed, classified orbital tracking data. In return, the company supplies tracking information from its stargaze system. That system draws on roughly 30,000 cameras mounted on starlink satellites. The data exchange covers both starlink

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:36.260
and star-shield spacecraft. Space X performs about 20,000 orbital adjustments each day,

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:40.140
roughly 1,000 of those adjustments address collision avoidance.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:45.860
The Pentagon data stays limited to internal operations and cannot support commercial products.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:51.200
John Herman, Senior Director for Starlink, noted that the satellites have limitations

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:57.200
detecting smaller objects. Rate our information therefore adds value alongside the optical

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:04.200
stargaze feed. SpaceX is discussing a $1.6 billion starlink contract with the Italian government.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:10.140
The talks focus on delivering high-speed connectivity nationwide through the existing constellation.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:17.000
Elon Musk stated high confidence that SpaceX will launch NVIDIA AI computers into orbit in

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:23.960
2027. Cursor published its 2026 monthly pricing tiers for the GROC integrated code editor.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:29.660
the tears cover both individual developers and teams. Space X posted four

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:35.780
positions for a planned future star-based facility in Louisiana. Starlink 32533

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:42.660
a V2 mini satellite reached orbit on October 30th, 2024 aboard a Falcon 9. It

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:48.580
maintains a stable altitude near 469 kilometers after more than 10,700

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:55.260
revolutions. The satellite's semi-major axis has varied by only about 200 meters across an 89

00:01:55.260 --> 00:02:01.880
day window. Net change stands at negative 64 meters, consistent with minor atmospheric drag.

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:11.500
Inclination remains between 53.177 and 53.183 degrees. Ground observations record a clear filter

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:18.420
magnitude of 7.48. Analysis points to SpaceX AI initiatives and the Starlink constellation

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:24.300
as ongoing growth drivers. Community trackers noted five Starlink satellites now undergoing

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:27.800
active deorbit as part of routine and of life procedures.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:33.220
Observers also highlighted that the new Space Force data agreement could affect daily

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:38.300
maneuver rates across the full constellation. One thing worth watching is the upcoming

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:43.900
Exploration of lockups on 328 million space X shares that become freely

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:48.920
tradable Thursday. That expiration coincides with an insider sale filing by

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:56.340
President and COO Gwynne Shotwell for 342,170 shares. Further lockup

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:02.940
explorations extend into 2027. On the AI front, Cursors 2026 pricing supports

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.980
wider developer access to GROC models through the editor.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.160
Musk's 2027 target for orbital and video systems

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.800
aligns with broader XAI and Starlink

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.380
compute integration plans.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:17.820
Recent analysis ties SpaceX AI work

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.680
and the Starlink network directly to company expansion.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:24.380
From an engineering standpoint, the StarGaze camera array

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:27.420
on Starlink satellites represents a distributed sensor

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:29.680
network that trades individual aperture

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:31.880
size for massive spatial coverage.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:38.180
Each of the roughly 30,000 cameras is small and low power, yet collectively they generate

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:43.380
a near real-time map of low-earth orbit objects down to size's radar often misses.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:49.660
The raw input cost is dominated by the silicon sensors, optics, and the incremental power

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:53.960
draw on satellites already carrying solar arrays sized for communications.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:59.960
Once the images are downlinked or processed locally, the system feeds directly into the

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:04.840
a daily collision avoidance pipeline that already executes about 1,000 avoidance maneuvers.

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:11.020
Adding classified radar tracks from the Pentagon closes the gap on smaller debris that optical

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:16.520
cameras struggle to detect at range, lowering the false negative rate without requiring larger

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:18.920
individual apertures or heavier satellites.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:24.660
The architecture therefore collapses the classic sensor trade-off between coverage and resolution

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:27.940
by turning the constellation itself into the instrument.

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:33.840
An approach whose marginal cost per additional data point falls as more satellites reach orbit.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:39.240
The marginal cost per additional data point continues to drop as constellation density

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:44.920
rises because each new satellite adds both communications capacity and sensing coverage

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:46.840
without separate dedicated hardware.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:49.120
